### BookVerse — manual API tests
### Requires the "REST Client" VS Code extension (or Thunder Client / Bruno).
### Click "Send Request" above each block. Cookies are stored & resent automatically.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:3000
@email = amr@example.com
@password = password123

### 1. Gateway health (no auth)
GET {{baseUrl}}/health

### 2. Signup (through the gateway) — sets sessionId + refreshToken cookies
# @name signup
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/signup
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Amr Nashaat",
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### 3. Login — capture the access token for protected calls below
# @name login
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

### grab the token out of the login response
@accessToken = {{login.response.body.data.tokens.accessToken}}

### ================================================================
### BOOK SERVICE
### ================================================================

### 4a. GET /books — PUBLIC (no token). Reads are open at the gateway. → 200
GET {{baseUrl}}/books

### 4b. POST /books — PROTECTED, valid token (matrix row 1). → 201
###     Gateway verifies the JWT and injects x-user-* downstream.
# @name createBook
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Distributed Systems for Skeptics",
  "description": "A field guide to partial failure.",
  "genre": ["non-fiction", "engineering"]
}

### capture the new book's id + its owner
@bookId = {{createBook.response.body.data.id}}
@bookOwnerId = {{createBook.response.body.data.authorId}}

### 4c. GET /books/:id — PUBLIC. → 200
GET {{baseUrl}}/books/{{bookId}}

### 4d. PATCH /books/:id — OWNER updating own book. → 200
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/books/{{bookId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Distributed Systems for Skeptics (2nd ed.)"
}

### 5. POST /books — PROTECTED, NO token (matrix row 2). → 401 at the GATEWAY
###    verifyJwt rejects before the request ever reaches book-service.
POST {{baseUrl}}/books
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Should never be created",
  "description": "no token",
  "genre": []
}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------
### 6. OWNERSHIP (fine-grained authz) — a DIFFERENT user tries to edit
###    the first user's book. → 403 (ForbiddenError). Authenticated, but not the owner.

### 6a. Second user signup
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/signup
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Mallory",
  "email": "mallory@example.com",
  "password": "password123"
}

### 6b. Second user login — capture their token
# @name login2
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "mallory@example.com",
  "password": "password123"
}
@accessToken2 = {{login2.response.body.data.tokens.accessToken}}

### 6c. Non-owner PATCH — passes authenticateUser, fails the ownership check. → 403
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/books/{{bookId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken2}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Hijacked title"
}

### 7. Refresh — uses the sessionId + refreshToken cookies automatically
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/refresh

### 8. Logout — clears the session
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/logout

### ================================================================
### DIRECT-TO-SERVICE bypass tests (run in a TERMINAL, not here —
### book-service is not published to the host, only on the docker net).
### ================================================================
###
### Matrix row 3 — direct call, NO gateway secret → 401
### (verifyGatewaySecret rejects before any route logic runs):
###
###   docker compose exec -T gateway wget -S -O- \
###     --header='content-type: application/json' \
###     --post-data='{"title":"Direct","description":"no secret","genre":["x"]}' \
###     http://book-service:3002/books/ 2>&1 | head -n 20
###
### Matrix row 4 — direct call, CORRECT secret + FORGED identity → 201.
### This is the lesson: the secret is the only gate, so a secret-holder is
### anyone. Note ALL THREE identity headers are forged (authenticateUser
### needs id+role+email); a partial forgery would 401 on validation, not auth.
### Secret is read from the container's env — never hardcoded here.
###
###   docker compose exec -T gateway sh -c 'wget -S -O- \
###     --header="content-type: application/json" \
###     --header="x-gateway-secret: $GATEWAY_SECRET" \
###     --header="x-user-id: 11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111" \
###     --header="x-user-role: USER" \
###     --header="x-user-email: forged@evil.test" \
###     --post-data="{\"title\":\"Forged\",\"description\":\"minted without the gateway\",\"genre\":[\"x\"]}" \
###     http://book-service:3002/books/' 2>&1 | head -n 30
###
### Then compare row 4's created authorId (11111111-…) against a real book's
### owner from 4b. The service cannot tell them apart.
###
### ---------------------------------------------------------------
### AUTH bypass (Lesson 01) — direct to auth-service with no secret → 401:
###
###   docker compose exec -T gateway wget -S -O- --post-data='{}' \
###     --header='Content-Type: application/json' \
###     http://auth-service:3001/auth/login 2>&1 | head
